    "q-74045": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74045",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Suppose ${b}_{2}=q{b}_{1}+a$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2}=-4.$ If $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and ${b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$, find the value of $q.$ \n\n*Hint: You might wish to take the equation ${b}_{2}=q{b}_{1}+a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}.$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, we take the equation ${b}_{2}=q{b}_{1}+a$ and find the dot product of both sides with ${b}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{2} & =q{b}_{1}+a \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (q{b}_{1}+a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & ={b}_{1}\\cdot (q{b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =q({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the formula for the dot product to compute the values of ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1} & ={b}_{1}^{2}+{b}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =9+16 \\\\  & =25 \\\\ {b}_{1}\\cdot a & ={b}_{1}{a}_{1}+{b}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3+8 \\\\  & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1},$ ${b}_{1}\\cdot a,$ and ${b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{2} & =q({b}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1})+({b}_{1}\\cdot a) \\\\ -4 & =q\\cdot 25+11 \\\\ -25q & =15 \\\\ q & =-\\frac{3}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
