    "q-74041": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74041",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d,$ if $c\\cdot a=-2,$ $c\\cdot b=4,$ and $d\\cdot b=5.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the fact that the dot product is linear in the first variable, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d & =(3a)\\cdot c-(4b)\\cdot c+(2b)\\cdot d \\\\  & =3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we can use the fact that the dot product is symmetric to get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(a\\cdot c)-4(b\\cdot c)+2(b\\cdot d) & =3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the values for $c\\cdot a,$ $c\\cdot b$ and $d\\cdot b,$ we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(c\\cdot a)-4(c\\cdot b)+2(d\\cdot b) & =3(-2)-4(4)+2(5) \\\\  & =-6-16+10 \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(3a-4b)\\cdot c+(2d)\\cdot b=-12.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
